.\"} . That is a single line (no actual newlines). The \\n sequences will be interpreted as newlines when parsed.\n\nThus, I will write the JSON object as a single line: {\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\\\n    nodes = case['nodes']; ...\"} But wait: earlier I said we need to write \\n (backslash n) as two characters. But in the JSON string, backslash must be escaped as \\\\ to be a literal backslash. However, \\n is an escape sequence for newline, not a literal backslash followed by n. So if I write \"def solve(case):\\n\" inside a JSON string, the JSON parser will interpret \\n as a newline character. That's correct. So I do not double the backslash. I just write \\n.\n\nBut I must be careful: When generating the JSON text, if I type the character backslash and n in my response, it will be interpreted as escape sequence in the text? We are writing plain text response. The response is a JSON object as text. So I need to include the two characters backslash and n exactly. In plain text, to type backslash, I can just type backslash. So I will write \\n.\n\nSo the final string will be exactly as I wrote above, but all on one line, with the double quotes escaped. I'll construct it.\n\nLet me write it in a single line:\n\n{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    core = case['core_distance']\\n    dist_list = case['distances']\\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"nodes must be list\\\")\\n    node_set = set(nodes)\\n    if any(k not in node_set for k in core):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"core_distance keys mismatch\\\")\\n    edges = []\\n    for u,v,d in dist_list:\\n        if u not in node_set or v not in node_set:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"node in distance not in nodes\\\")\\n        w = max(core[u], core[v], d)\\n        if u > v:\\n            u, v = v, u\\n        edges.append((w, u, v))\\n    edges.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1], x[2]))\\n    parent = {n: n for n in nodes}\\n    rank = {n: 0 for n in nodes}\\n    def find(x):\\n        while parent[x] != x:\\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\\n            x = parent[x]\\n        return x\\n    def union(x, y):\\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\\n        if rx == ry:\\n            return False\\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\\n            parent[rx] = ry\\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n        else:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n            rank[rx] += 1\\n        return True\\n    mst_edges = []\\n    total_weight = 0.0\\n    for w, u, v in edges:\\n        if union(u, v):\\n            mst_edges.append([u, v, w])\\n            total_weight += w\\n            if len(mst_edges) == len(nodes) - 1:\\n                break\\n    if len(mst_edges) != len(nodes) - 1 and len(nodes) > 1:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"graph not connected\\\")\\n    return {\\\"mst_edges\\\": mst_edges, \\\"total_weight\\\": total_weight}\"}\n\nNow, this is a single line? It has actual newlines in my typing because I hit enter. But in the actual response, I must not have newlines in the JSON string except escaped. So I need to remove all actual newlines from the above and replace them with \\n sequences. But I already used \\n in the text. Actually in the above, after each colon and newline, I wrote a newline character in the thinking text, but that is not part of the JSON string. In the JSON string, the \\n is the two-character escape. So I need to write the entire string with no actual newlines between the lines. I'll concatenate everything into one line. I'll copy the code and join with \\n. But since I'm writing in text, I can just ensure there are no newline characters outside the \\n escapes. I'll write the JSON object as a single line.\n\nHowever, to make it readable, I'll write it with explicit \\n separators. The final response must be exactly one JSON object. I'll produce it as a single line. I'll use a Python script to generate it? But I'll write manually.\n\nLet me write the implementation string as a single string with \\n:\n\n\"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    core = case['core_distance']\\n    dist_list = case['distances']\\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"nodes must be list\\\")\\n    node_set = set(nodes)\\n    if any(k not in node_set for k in core):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"core_distance keys mismatch\\\")\\n    edges = []\\n    for u,v,d in dist_list:\\n        if u not in node_set or v not in node_set:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"node in distance not in nodes\\\")\\n        w = max(core[u], core[v], d)\\n        if u > v:\\n            u, v = v, u\\n        edges.append((w, u, v))\\n    edges.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1], x[2]))\\n    parent = {n: n for n in nodes}\\n    rank = {n: 0 for n in nodes}\\n    def find(x):\\n        while parent[x] != x:\\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\\n            x = parent[x]\\n        return x\\n    def union(x, y):\\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\\n        if rx == ry:\\n            return False\\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\\n            parent[rx] = ry\\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n        else:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n            rank[rx] += 1\\n        return True\\n    mst_edges = []\\n    total_weight = 0.0\\n    for w, u, v in edges:\\n        if union(u, v):\\n            mst_edges.append([u, v, w])\\n            total_weight += w\\n            if len(mst_edges) == len(nodes) - 1:\\n                break\\n    if len(mst_edges) != len(nodes) - 1 and len(nodes) > 1:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"graph not connected\\\")\\n    return {\\\"mst_edges\\\": mst_edges, \\\"total_weight\\\": total_weight}\"\n\nNow, this should be the entire string. I'll put it inside the JSON object. Also ensure there are no trailing spaces.\n\nNow I will output the JSON."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2021,"completion_tok